Abstract
A mesoporous octahedral copper oxide@titania $\mathrm{(CuO/TiO_{2})}$ ) composites with core-shelled structure have been successfully fabricated via a facile and cost-effective approach, which involves two main steps: the creation of homogeneous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell onto the octahedral Cu-based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) template and thermal decomposition of the template at controlled temperature in the air. The design of combining CuO with the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer within a porous octahedra structure is beneficial to integrate the advantages of different components and address the severe volume change associated with pulverization issue that exists in most metal oxides-based electrodes. When assembled as an anode material for lithium-ion batteries, the as-fabricated mesoporous $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedra can achieve outstanding electrochemical performance in terms of a high reversible capacity $(692\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for over 200 cycles) and exceptional rate capability (441 and 387 mA h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1600 and $3200\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , respectively).
## 1. Introduction
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have attracted considerable attention and increasing interest due to the fact that they are the most utilized power source for portable electronic devices and electric vehicles. Nevertheless, the widely used graphite anode in commercial LIBs has relatively low theoretical capacity of only 372 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and poor rate performance due to safety issue, which would not be capable to meet the rapidly rising demand for high energy density/power density batteries [1–5]. Transition metal oxides are believed to be a promising alternative to graphite due to their high specific capacity, resource abundance and design flexibility [6–14]. Particularly, copper oxides $\left(\mathrm{CuO}\right)$ have been actively investigated owing to their high specific energy storage capacity (670 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) via a two electron transfer process, improved safety than graphite and moderate cost [15–19]. Unfortunately, CuO electrodes inevitably suffer from irreversible capacity loss, unsatisfactory cycling stability and poor rate capability caused by the huge volume expansion and severe pulverization of the electrode during repeated charge-discharge process, which has prevented it from practical applications.
To overcome these obstacles, one feasible strategy is to construct porous architectures assembled by nanoparticles [20–24]. Within this structure, the micro/nano-scaled architectural skeleton not only effectively avoid the aggregation of active nanoparticles but also provide appropriate space for accommodating volume changes, and thus promote the kinetics of lithium ions storage. Meanwhile, the assembled nanoparticles can reduce the diffusion distance of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions and are benefit to increasing additional reaction sites for lithium intercalation/deintercalation, enhancing the rate capability. Nonetheless, in most cases, these porous architectures still exhibit capacity fading during cycling due to the poor electrical conductivity and weak structural integrity caused by the repeated volume variation of nanoparticles and the successive generation of a solid electrolyte interphase (SEI). In this regard, designing core-shell structural composites consisting of metal oxides as “core” and coating layer as “shell” is a promising strategy, which has been employed to develop high-performance anodes for LIBs [25,26]. The coated layer not only serves as a buffering space that alleviates the volume expansion but also contributes additional capacity as well as synergistic advantages for battery electrodes.
On the other hand, as an important insertion-type electrode material, titania $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ has a high lithium insertion-extraction potential and a trivial volume change during the cycling process, which is usually considered as a suitable coating materials for metal oxides and potentially beneficial to both the structural stability and the electrochemical performance of the composites [27–31]. For example, Fu et al. prepared porous hollow iron oxide $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right)$ coated with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}/\mathrm{Ti}{\bf O}_{2}$ ) core-shell nanospheres through a carbon-template assisted approach and found that they exhibited excellent electrochemical performance with improved cyclability and rate capability in comparison to bare hollow $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ spheres [27]. Chen et al. reported that porous cubic $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}@\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ could deliver a capacity of $936~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles, superior to that of $\bf{M n}_{2}\bf{O}_{3}$ $(443\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}})$ [28]. Geng et al. explored mesoporous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanorods with uniform $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ coating which showed a high capacity of 803 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $200\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles [30]. Despite these advances, the reported products typically involve a complicated and tedious procedure, which increases the difficulty to control the homogeneity of hybrid composites, especially for the well-defined $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ architectures.
In this work, we report a facile method to synthesize mesoporous $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ core-shell structure consisting of an inner core of octahedron-like CuO and an outer shell of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by a metal-organic framework (MOF)-template strategy. Firstly, the Cu-based MOF (HKUST-1) was chosen as the template. Noted that HKSUT-1 is another name of the Cu-based MOF consisting of Cu(II)-paddlewheel-type nodes and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate struts and it was prepared by Williams et al. from Hong Kong University of Science and Technology (HKUST) in 1999 [32]. Secondly, titanium isopropoxide (TTIP), an organic titanium alkoxide, was used as the precursor for creating TiO2 layer. The titanium alkoxide showed very good wettability on the surface of HKUST-1 particles, leading to a uniform coating of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ after hydrolysis. Lastly, HKUST-1 particles coated with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer were thermally converted into mesoporous $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedra. Benefiting from the unique structural design, such porous core-shell architectures are expected to exhibit enhanced electrochemical performance with both high reversible capacity and superior rate capability. When investigated as an anode material in LIBs, $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composites exhibited high specific capacity of $\sim692\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ up to 500 cycles and exceptional rate performance (441 and $387\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1.6 and $3.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Preparation of HKUST-1 template
The preparation of HKSUT-1 templates is similar to previous report with a modified procedure [16]. Briefly, $2.0\mathrm{~g~}$ copper nitrate $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , Merck, ≥ $99.5~\%$ , Germany) was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{ml}$ methanol (Merck, $\geq99.5~\%$ , Germany) to form a clear solution, which was subsequently added into $80~\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol solution containing $3.5\mathrm{~g~}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{C_{6}H_{3}}(\mathrm{COOH})_{3}$ , Merck, $\geq95.0~\%$ , Germany) and $\boldsymbol{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP, average molar weight 40, 000, Sigma-Aldrich, USA). After thorough mixing and incubation at room temperature for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the formed blue precipitates were centrifuged, washed with methanol for three times, and finally dried in oven at ${}60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
2.2. Synthesis of mesoporous core-shell CuO@TiO2 composites
$0.20~\mathrm{g}$ of as-prepared HKUST-1 particles were dispersed into ethanol solution (20 ml), followed by adding $0.3\mathrm{\ml}$ of titanium isopropoxide (TTIP, Sigma-Aldrich, $297.0\%$ , USA) into the solution. After sonicated for 10 minutes, diluted hydrochloric acid (0.5 mL, $\mathrm{pH}=2$ ) was dropwise added and the hydrolysis reaction of TTIP was proceeded for overnight at room temperature under magnetic stirring. The formed core-shell HKUST- $\boldsymbol{1}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles were centrifuged and washed with ethanol, and then dried in oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, the desiccated HKUST- $1/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ powder was heated to $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at $1^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ using tube furnace in air and kept at this temperature for 2 hours to obtain mesoporous $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedra.
## 2.3. Characterization
The composition and phase of the products were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD) (Bruker D8 Advance with Cu Kα radiation). The morphology and microstructure of products were characterized by a field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, JEOL JSM-7600F), a high resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM, JEM-2100F) system attached with energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectroscopy. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of products were measured by nitrogen adsorption-desorption at 77 K (Quantachrome Instruments Autosorb AS-6B instrument).
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical measurements of the as-prepared products were conducted via coin-type cells. The working electrode was obtained by mixing $70\mathrm{\mt}\%$ active materials, $20\mathrm{wt}\%$ Super-P, and 10 wt% polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) dissolved in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone solvent (Aldrich, $99\%$ ). The well mixture was then coated onto a copper foil and dried under vacuum at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ before processing. The cells were assembled in an argon-filled glovebox by using a metallic Li foil as the combined counter and reference electrodes, and Celgard 2400 membrane as separators. The solution of $1\mathrm{MLiPF_{6}}$ in a mixture of ethylene carbonate and dimethyl carbonate (1:1 in volume) was used as electrolyte. The assembled cells were aged for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and then tested on a Land-CT2001A battery test system within the voltage range of 0.01−3.0 V vs Li+/Li. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were recorded on an electrochemical workstation (AutoLab PGSTAT302N) with a frequency range from100 KHz to $10\mathrm{mHz}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 illustrates the schematic synthesis strategy of mesoporous CuO/TiO2 core-shell octahedra. Cu-based metal-organic frameworks (HKUST-1) with octahedral shape are first synthesized by a simple co-precipitation reaction between copper nitrate and benzene tri-carboxylic acid at room temperature. The as-synthesized HKUST-1 is then coated with a thin layer of titanium isopropoxide (TTIP) precursor by a sol–gel process, forming HKUST- $1/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ octahedral composites. The composites were calcined in air, during which the Cu ions in HKUST-1 were oxidized to CuO and the coordinated organic ligands were decomposed, while the coated amorphous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was crystallized into anatase phase TiO2, leading to the $\mathbf{CuO}@\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ core-shell octahedra.
Fig. 1.  Schematic illustration of the process for synthesis of porous $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ core-shell octahedra.
Fig. 2a shows a low-magnification field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) image of HKUST-1 templates (Fig. 2a). The obtained templates are composed of monodispersed particles with size ranging from 200 to $500~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ Magnified FESEM image in Fig. 2b reveals that these particles have octahedral morphology with a very smooth surface. The powder X-ray diffraction (XRD)
patterns of HKUST-1 and its simulated results were shown in Fig. S1 (Supporting Information), from which all the diffraction peaks in the $2\theta$ range of $5{-}40^{\circ}$ are agreement with the published results [33]. Fig.2c and d show the FESEM images of $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ core-shell composites, indicating that they retained well the octahedral morphology of the HKUST-1 template. Nevertheless, the surfaces of composites are very rough, implying the formation of porous structure.
Fig. 3a presents the Rietveld refinement of powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ core-shell octahedra and the refined result is listed in Table S1. The XRD patterns can be well indexed to a monoclinic $\mathtt{C u O}$ phase $(91\mathrm{\mt.\%})$ and a tetragonal anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (9 wt. $\%$ ), indicating that the HKUST- $1/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ completely converted into $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ composites. The chemical compositions and metal oxidation states of composites were further studied by $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Fig.3b, the $\mathrm{Cu}2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum contains a main $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak at 933.2 eV and two shake-up satellites peaks (941.5 and $943.9\ \mathrm{{\eV}},$ ) with higher binding values, demonstrating the existence of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ from the $\mathtt{C u O}$ phase [17]. Fig. 3c shows the Ti 2p spectrum, in which the two prominent peaks around 464.7 and 458.7 eV correspond to the Ti 2p3/2 and Ti 2p1/2 spin-orbit peaks of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , respectively. The main peak of O 1s observed at $531.5~\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to the binding energy value of Cu-O [34], while another peak appeared at $529.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ may originate from the mixture of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and CuO (Fig. 3d).
Fig. 3. (a) Rietveld refinement of XRD patterns of $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ core-shell octahedra, high-resolution of (b) $\mathrm{Cu}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ , (c) Ti 2p and (d) O 1s XPS spectra of $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ core-shell octahedra.
The microstructure of $\mathbf{CuO}@\mathbf{TiO}_{2}$ core-shell octahedra composites was further characterized by transmission electron microscope (TEM). In agreement with the FESEM findings, octahedra-like structures with edge lengths ranging from 300 to 500 nm were observed (Fig. 4a). The magnified TEM image (Fig. 4b) further reveals that this octahedral structure was porous and consisted of a large number of nanoparticles. The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image in Fig. 4c displays clear lattice fringes with the $d_{}$ -spacings of 0.27 and $0.35\ \mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (110) plane of monoclinic CuO phase and the (101) plane of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ phase, respectively. The elemental mapping results confirm the co-existence and uniform dispersion of Cu, Ti and O elements (Figs. 4d–g).
TEM images, (c) HRTEM image, (d)-(g) energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping images. The inset in (d) showing the corresponding geometrical mode of an octahedron.
The porosity and surface area of the core-shell structures were measured by N2 sorption method. Fig. 5 displays a typical type IV behavior, indicating a rich existence of mesoporous. Correspondingly, the core-shell $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ composites  have a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of $88.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , much larger than that of the previously reported CuO-based nanostructures [16]. The pore size distribution calculated from the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) equation (inset of Fig. 5) revealed abundant mesoporous ranging from 1 to $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the average pore width ${\sim}4.2~\mathrm{nm}$ . The large BET surface area of CuO/TiO2 composites as well as their suitable pores distribution may be beneficial for enhancing the mass diffusion of electrolyte and buffering volume changes of the active materials during the electrochemical reaction.
To demonstrate the advantage of the as-prepared mesoporous core-shell $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedral composites, we assembled coin-type cells using composites as anode materials for LIBs. Fig.6a gives the first three consecutive cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the electrodes prepared by porous core-shell $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ octahedra at a scan rate of $0.1\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ . In the first cathodic scan curve, three peaks around 1.2, 0.8, and $0.68\mathrm{V}$ can be observed, corresponding to the reduction of intermediate phase to $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the further reduction to metallic Cu and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , respectively [31]. A weak broad peak at $1.68{\mathrm{V}}$ can be attributed to the overlapping of the reduction peaks of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and CuO, which is similar to the previous report [27]. In the anodic scan, the peak at 2.41 V is associated with the oxidation of Cu particles into $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and subsequent conversion into CuO. From the second cycle onwards, cathodic peaks shift to higher potentials, while the anodic peak position remains almost unchanged. The CV curves overlapped well after the first cycle, indicating a good cycling stability of the porous CuO/TiO2 octahedra electrode. Fig. 6b shows the representative galvanostatic discharge-charge curves of $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedra electrode in the first, second and fifth cycle at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ . The $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedra anode could deliver an initial discharge and charge capacities of 1092 and 780 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, corresponding to a Coulombic efficiency of $71.4\%$ . The initial irreversible capacity loss was probably caused by the decomposition of electrolyte or some irreversible processes such as the formation of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) layer. Fig. 6c shows the discharge capacity as a function of cycle number at a current density of 100 mA $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . After a few charge-discharge cycles, the porous core-shell $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electrode exhibits excellent cyclic capacity retention and their Coulombic efficiency steadily maintains at ${\sim}98\%$ . At the end of 200 cycles, a reversible discharge capacity of $692\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is still retained. On the contrary, without the protection of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell, the porous $\mathtt{C u O}$ electrode shows a quick decrease in capacity and remains only 395 $\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles.
Fig. 6. (a) Cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the initial 3 cycles for the $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ electrode scanned at the rate of $0.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , (b) galvanostatic charge–discharge voltage profiles of the $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode at a current density of $0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , (c) comparative cycling performance of $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ and pure $\mathtt{C u O}$ at a current density of $0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and the corresponding Coulombic efficiency, (d) rate capabilities of the $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and pure $\mathtt{C u O}$ at various current densities.
Table 1 has summarized some recent works on CuO-based nanostructures as anodes for LIBs, from which the cycling performance of our $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composite electrode is also superior to most of the previously reported CuO-based electrodes. In addition to the high reversible capacity and remarkable cycling stability, the porous core-shell $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ electrodes also exhibit superior rate capability. As shown in Fig. 6d, the average capacities of $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ are 683, 636, 558, 509, 441 and 387 mA h g– at current densities of 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1.6, and $3.2\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , respectively, much higher than those of $\mathtt{C u O}$ electrodes (495, 405, 351, 294, 225 and $152\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at current densities of 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1.6, and $3.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively). After deep cycling at a high current of up to $3.2\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , a capacity of $675\mathrm{\mA}\hat{\mathbf{h}}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}\mathrm{\c}$ an still be recovered by using a small current density of 0.1 A $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , demonstrating that porous $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ octahedral composites have a great potential as a high-rate electrode material in LIBs.
To gain insight into the enhanced electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ hybrid composites for LIBs, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted. As shown in Fig.7a, the Nyquist plots consist of a depressed semicircle whose diameter represents the charge transfer resistance at the high frequency region and a straight line the low frequency region in association with the lithium ion diffusion in the electrolyte. Compared with the CuO electrode, the $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode shows a semicircle with a smaller diameter and the appreciably steep slope line in the low frequency region, indicating that the coating of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can effectively improve the charge transfer and accelerate the lithium-ion intercalation/deintercalation of CuO. The morphology of the $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ electrode after 200 cycles were also investigated by TEM (Fig.7b). It is found that the octahedral morphology is well preserved, indicating a high structural stability of $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composites. The excellent electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ composites can be attributed to a synergistic effect in their physical/structural features. Firstly, the composed nanosized subunits of $\mathtt{C u O}$ would reduce the ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions diffusion pathway and enhance the kinetics of charge carrier. Secondly, the porous structure is beneficial to electrolyte penetration and can alleviate the repeated large volume variation caused by lithium insertion/extraction as well as the strain accumulated in the CuO particles. Thirdly, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ coating can be served as a buffer layer to restrain the volume expansion due to its unexceptional structure stability and maintain the structural int(eag)rity, further improving the cycling stability.(b)
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a facile and feasible MOF-template approach has been developed to synthesize the mesoporous core-shelled $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ octahedra composites. The approach involves the creation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell onto the octahedral HKUST-1 core by a sol-gel process and then transformation of HKUST- $1/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{CuO}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ by a calcination process. The as-synthesized $\mathrm{CuO/TiO}_{2}$ composites used as anodes in LIBs exhibit high reversible capacity and excellent cycling stability (692 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 100 mA $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for over 200 cycles), much better than those of bare CuO and previously reported CuO-based nanostructures. The excellent electrochemical performance can be ascribed to a synergetic effect between CuO and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and the unique feature of porous core-shell structure. We believe that the proposed synthetic methodology may provide a new pathway for constructing high-performance metal oxides-based anodes
in LIBs.
